<observation>
{"source_id": "W2593975868", "title": "An Investigation of Note-Taking and Review on Test Performance", "year": 2016, "doi": "https://doi.org/10.7916/d8qz2gpq", "abstract": "The present study investigated the effects of note-taking and note review on test performance. Participants viewed a lecture and took notes either longhand or by laptop, then either reviewed their notes or did not before completing a test of factual and conceptual learning. Laptop note takers recorded more notes than longhand note takers. There was no significant main effect of note-taking medium on test performance, and no significant medium by review interaction, indicating that reviewing notes did not differentially benefit longhand versus laptop note takers."}
</observation>{"thought": "I'll fetch the 2025 collaborative review study to see whether it reports a medium-by-review interaction.", "action": "fetch_source", "args": {"source_id": "W4415356838"}}

<observation>
{"source_id": "W4415356838", "title": "Collaborative review enhances note-taking, especially after a longer delay, but does not boost test performance", "year": 2025, "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1080/09658211.2025.2573272", "abstract": "The present study replicated and extended prior research by comparing the effects of reviewing notes in groups vs. reviewing notes individually on individual final test performance. We also examined the potential interaction between reviewing notes and note-taking medium (longhand vs. laptop). Participants took notes longhand or by laptop and reviewed them either collaboratively or individually. Reviewing notes did not boost test performance overall, and there was no significant interaction between review condition and note-taking medium, suggesting that review does not alter the longhand versus laptop comparison."}
